      ":question/problem": "Which of the numbers below belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$ is the set of all positive real numbers and zero.\n\n- $-0.9=-\\frac{9}{10}$: It is negative, and it is a rational number but not an integer, since it can be written as $-\\frac{9}{10}$. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $-\\sqrt{19}$: It is negative, and it is an irrational real number. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $-\\frac{4}{4}=-1$: It is negative, and it is an integer. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$.\n\n- $-\\frac{10}{4}=-\\frac{5}{2}$: It is negative, and it is a rational number but not an integer. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{R}_0^{+}$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is: None of those listed.",
